<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joscelin II of Edessa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1159">1159</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joscelin II was the last count of Edessa, succeeding his father Joscelin I in 1131. While he was absent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> took the city of Edessa on 24 December 1144, which prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. Joscelin failed to retake it in 1146, was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s forces in 1150 and died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa.jpg" alt="Joscelin II of Edessa" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous 13th-century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Histoire_dOutremer-Boulogne-Ms142-f153v_(cropped_Raymond_of_Poitiers_Count_of_Antioch_and_Josselin_II_Count_of_Edessa_playing_chess).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joscelin II was born around 1113 in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, where his father, Joscelin I, had gone after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Bourcq</a> deprived him of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turbessel">Turbessel</a>. His mother was Beatrice, daughter of the Armenian ruler Constantine. After the elder Joscelin helped Baldwin of Bourcq to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, he received Edessa. Joscelin II was captured and exchanged in the wars that followed, and in 1131 his father was mortally wounded besieging a castle northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, dying soon after the siege of Kaisun was lifted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As count Joscelin first sided with Princess Alice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> against King Fulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He and Fulk won the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin">Qinnasrin</a> in 1133 against Sawar, a lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>. When the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> entered the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> in 1137, Joscelin swore fealty, but he held back during the siege of Shaizar in 1138, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a>, prince of Antioch, became his overlord around 1140.</p><p>In 1143 Zengi renewed his war on Edessa. While Joscelin marched to aid his ally Kara Arslan, Zengi broke into Edessa on 24 December 1144 and into the citadel two days later, killing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and sparing local Christians. Joscelin kept the half of the county west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. After Zengi was murdered on 14 September 1146, Joscelin entered the city on 27 October but could not take the citadel. Zengi's son Nur ad-Din routed him on 3 November, and the city was left desolate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1150 Nur ad-Din's forces captured Joscelin as he travelled to take up a regency; he was blinded and imprisoned, and the county was divided among its neighbours. He died in captivity in May 1159, having refused conversion, and received his last sacraments from Ignace, the Jacobite Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> portrayed him as idle and dissolute, and many 20th-century historians followed that view. Malcolm Barber suggests William's dislike of Joscelin III coloured it, and Nicholas Morton holds that the county was not in decline before 1144.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joscelin II of Edessa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nur ad-Din's madrasa and hospital in Damascus and his mosque at Mosul remained in use, and his fortification of the citadel of Aleppo, where <b>Joscelin II of Edessa</b> and Baldwin II of Jerusalem were held, shaped one of the great castles of the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its counts, Baldwin II, Joscelin I and <b>Joscelin II</b>, were exposed on every side; the defeat at Harran in 1104 halted their advance towards Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-edessa">County of Edessa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1149" aria-label="Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Joscelin II of Edessa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5493 4455h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5493 4455h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1149">What was here in 1149? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
